window.docContents[48].resolve({"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Inviscid-Boussinesq-Flow":{"contents":"\n\nThe inviscid Boussinesq system couples incompressible momentum to an advected\nscalar. In the vertical-buoyancy convention used here,\n\n\n\nChen and Hou introduce the upper-half-plane vorticity–streamfunction system\nand its dynamic rescaling in §2.1, equations (2.3)–(2.7)\n. We keep that\nformulation separate from the standard primitive velocity–pressure equations:\nthe buoyancy orientation and scalar-vorticity sign are explicit. A checked\ncurl calculation identifies exactly which primitive convention yields the\npaper's vorticity source. The dynamic rescaling of the upper-half-plane\nequations, its normalization, and an independent fixed-power primitive\ncalculation are checked as well, and the half-plane Green potential inverts the\nPoisson problem for compactly supported  sources with an odd extension\nacross the boundary.\n\nThe scalar drives rotation through its horizontal variation. With the\ncounterclockwise convention\n, taking curl of the displayed\nbuoyancy force gives . A spatially constant temperature\nhas zero curl and its force can be absorbed into the pressure by replacing\n with . For sufficiently smooth fields, differentiating\nthe transport equation gives\n\n\n\nTemperature is constant along each particle path, yet its gradient can grow\nas neighboring paths stretch and compress. That growing gradient feeds the\nvorticity equation, whose velocity in turn transports the temperature.\nThe feedback explains why the transported scalar remains dynamically active.\nReversing the curl or buoyancy convention reverses the corresponding source\nsign.\n\nA classical Boussinesq field consists of velocity , temperature ,\nand pressure . On the chosen set of times all three fields are jointly\n, their relevant slices are classically differentiable, and the three\ndisplayed equations hold pointwise. The impermeable boundary condition is a\nseparate predicate, not part of the whole-space model. Velocity is an unknown\nhere, not reconstructed from vorticity or a half-plane streamfunction.\n\nFor the rescaling calculation, it is useful to eliminate pressure and recover\nvelocity from a streamfunction. With  regularity, its mixed partials cancel\nin the divergence, while taking curl leaves a Laplacian with a sign determined\nby our convention. Recovering the streamfunction from vorticity then requires\nsolving a Poisson problem in the half-plane.\n\nFor a planar streamfunction , defineThe counterclockwise scalar curl of a planar velocity is\n; the clockwise curl\n is the convention of the paper.\n\nFor a  streamfunction, the velocity is incompressible, its standard\nscalar curl is , and its clockwise scalar curl is .\n\nExpand the two velocity components. The divergence is\n, which vanishes by\nthe  mixed-partial theorem. Expanding either scalar curl leaves the two\ndiagonal second derivatives with the corresponding sign.\n\nWith the sign fixed, the paper's system can be stated in the three scalar\nunknowns : the clockwise vorticity is transported and\ndriven by , the temperature is transported, and the\nstreamfunction is tied to  by a Dirichlet problem on the upper\nhalf-plane. Velocity appears only as .\n\nOn , the physical equations areThe boundary trace is . The fields are defined on the whole\nplane. Vorticity and temperature are jointly  on the admitted times,\nwith ambient time differentiability there; every streamfunction slice is\nspatially . No joint time regularity of the streamfunction is required.\nThe equations are imposed only in the open upper half-plane.\n\nThe definition stores  and the zero trace as separate\nequations.\n\nThe streamfunction equation identifies  with the clockwise curl of\n; the reconstructed velocity is incompressible; and a differentiable zero\nstreamfunction trace implies zero normal velocity at .\n\nThe first two claims are the planar streamfunction identities. For the boundary\nclaim, restrict  to the horizontal coordinate line. Its trace is the\nconstant zero function, so its tangential derivative \nvanishes. This derivative is precisely the normal velocity component.\n\nThe primitive and vorticity-stream formulations are related by taking the\nclockwise curl of the momentum equation. The pressure drops out,\nincompressibility turns the curl of the convective term into\n, and the buoyancy  contributes\n; matching this with the paper's source\n is what pins down the buoyancy orientation.\n\nAssume  is jointly  on spacetime and, at the time considered,\n and  are respectively  and  on the whole plane.\nIf the primitive momentum force is  and clockwise\nvorticity is , then on every open\nspatial domain where the primitive momentum equation and incompressibility\nhold,Thus the paper's positive source is the downward-buoyancy choice\n. Under that convention, with a supplied  streamfunction\nsatisfying  and the zero boundary\ntrace, a primitive solution with jointly continuously differentiable temperature\nsolving both the momentum equation with force  and the temperature\ntransport equation induces the full upper-half-plane vorticity–streamfunction solution predicate.\n\nTake clockwise curl locally inside the open spatial domain. Equality on a\nneighborhood gives equality of the required coordinate derivatives, while\nmixed derivatives cancel the pressure curl. For a divergence-free planar\nvelocity, the curl of the convective term is ; the\nvertical force contributes . The streamfunction identities then provide\nincompressibility and , while the primitive scalar\ntransport equation and zero trace give the remaining solution fields and\nboundary condition.\n\nSelf-similar blow-up is studied by rescaling space, time, and the amplitudes of\n and  with time-dependent factors, with the aim that a\nsolution collapsing to the origin becomes one converging to a stationary\nprofile. The factors are not free: the streamfunction scale is forced by\n, and transforming the source  requires\nthe amplitude relation .\n\nThe sign of the length rate determines whether these coordinates shrink or\nexpand in physical space. The\nDe Gregorio blowup in the companion repository BlowupZoo gives an expanding\nreal-line example with the corresponding physical clock.\n\nAt rescaled time , setThe scale laws are ,\n, , and\n, together with  and\n, with  so dilation preserves the upper\nhalf-plane. The complete transformed-system predicate reuses\nthe canonical pair of rescaled scalar equations and adds the streamfunction\nPoisson equation and zero boundary trace.\n\nAfter the change of variables, the rescaled scalar fields obey equations of the\nsame transport type as before, with two modifications: the transport velocity\nacquires the dilation term , and each equation gains a linear amplitude\nterm with coefficient  or .\n\nFor rate functions , the rescaled transport velocity isThe scalar part of the rescaled system is the pair of pointwise equationsThe equations are stated for arbitrary rate functions, independently of any\nparticular scaling data, and may be imposed either at one time or on a\nspecified space-time domain. The two residual functions are the left side\nminus the right side of the corresponding equation.\n\nWriting each equation as a residual makes exact solutions and approximate\nprofiles comparable: an exact solution has zero residual, whereas the paper's\napproximate profile has a small residual that must be estimated in a weighted\nspace.\n\nEach rescaled scalar equation holds on a domain exactly when its pointwise\nresidual vanishes throughout that domain.\n\nExpand the residual as the left side minus the right side and rearrange.\n\nThe equations are imposed only in the open half-plane, yet the normalization\nreads off derivatives at the origin, which lies on the boundary. For\nfields smooth across the boundary the gap is closed by continuity: an identity\nbetween continuous functions that holds on a set persists on its closure, and\nthe same argument applies to its spatial derivatives.\n\nFor globally continuously differentiable representatives, either rescaled scalar equation holding\non a spatial domain also holds on its closure. If the domain is open, then\nwith one further degree of regularity for each spatial differentiation, the\nonce-differentiated vorticity equation and twice-differentiated temperature\nequation extend to the closure as well. This applies in particular to the open\nupper half-plane.Writing ,\n,\n, and\n, the conclusions are\n\nBoth sides of each interior identity are continuous. Equality therefore\npasses to the closure. On an open domain, the differentiated statements follow\nby first differentiating the equality and then applying the same closure\nargument to the continuous derivative fields.\n\nThe rescaled equations become a theorem rather than a definition once the scale\nlaws are imposed. Differentiating the pullbacks by the chain rule, the dilation\nterm comes from , the amplitude terms from  and\n, and the physical time derivative enters through ;\nthe amplitude compatibility is what makes the transformed source match the\ntransformed advection terms.\n\nA classical upper-half-plane solution on an open physical-time set satisfies\nthe following transformed system at every rescaled time whose physical-time\nimage belongs to that set and at which the scale laws hold:with the zero streamfunction boundary trace preserved.\n\nDifferentiate the three pullbacks. The derivative of  contributes\n, which cancels advection by . The reconstructed velocity\nhas scale . Vorticity advection and the physical\nvorticity time-derivative term both carry ; temperature advection\ncarries . The source derivative carries ,\nequal to  by compatibility. The streamfunction scale contributes\n, preserving the Poisson equation.\nPositive  preserves the upper half-plane, and the boundary trace remains\nzero under tangential dilation.\n\nThe normalization also relies on symmetry: the paper's profile has odd\nvorticity and even temperature under reflection in the vertical axis. Vorticity\nparity does not automatically pass to the streamfunction, because  is\ndetermined by  only through the Dirichlet problem. The reflected and\nnegated streamfunction solves the same problem, so uniqueness in a suitable\nclass transfers the parity.\n\nLet  and let  be an admissible class of\nwhole-plane streamfunctions, closed under .\nSuppose  is  on the whole plane and the Dirichlet\nproblem is unique in . ThenThe theorem keeps  explicit. Uniqueness among all smooth\nfunctions on the unbounded half-plane would be false without a decay, energy,\nor representation condition.\n\nThe Laplacian is invariant under , so  solves the same\nPoisson equation as . It has the same zero boundary trace and remains\nin  by reflection stability. Uniqueness in  gives\n.\n\nUniqueness is an assumption in the previous theorem. When the streamfunction is\ngiven explicitly by the half-plane Green function, the same parity can instead\nbe read off from the kernel: reflection preserves the half-plane and the\nmethod-of-images kernel, so oddness of the source transfers to the potential\ndirectly.\n\nIf  is odd under\n and its method-of-images Green integrand is\nintegrable at every field point, thenis odd under  and has zero horizontal boundary trace. These conclusions\nare proved from kernel geometry. Normal oddness together with integrability of\n identifies the same integral with the whole-space\nconvolution, without compact support. If, in addition,  is a\ncompactly supported  whole-space function, then the integral is \nand satisfies  on the upper half-plane. The approximate\nprofile of the paper has a nonintegrable \nfar field, which Section 7.2.1 treats by a semi-analytic homogeneous\nstreamfunction and an angular Dirichlet ODE before estimating a\nfaster-decaying numerical remainder.\n\nApply the generic half-space potential theorem to reflection in coordinate\nzero. This reflection preserves ; kernel invariance and the\nmeasure-preserving change of variables transfer oddness of  to\n. Boundary cancellation is pointwise in the source variable before\nintegration. Under the normal odd-extension hypothesis, the planar\nmethod-of-images theorem identifies the potential with \nwhenever that convolution is integrable. The compact  inversion theorem\nthen proves the pointwise Poisson equation.\n\nThe semi-analytic far-field ansatz in Section 7.2.1 is governed by the exact\npolar identity\n\n\n\nThus the angular Dirichlet equation\n produces the uncut source\n at positive radii. Lean proves this homogeneous\npolar calculation. It also proves the exact commutator introduced by the\nradial cutoff : the extra terms involve only\n and . A general  pullback theorem\nidentifies this polar operator with the Cartesian Euclidean Laplacian away\nfrom . These identities are the\nhomogeneous polar\nLaplacian theorem.\n\nLet  be continuous, set , and\nsuppose . With , there is a classical solutionThe construction keeps the resonance condition explicit. DefineThenHere , so  and the denominator is nonzero.\nContinuous forcing gives . If  on , with\n, the formula also gives the explicit sufficient bound for :On the same interval, its first derivative satisfiesThe homogeneous streamfunction  therefore has\nnegative polar Laplacian  for .\nA certified residual gives a quantitative correction as well. Let \nhave exact zero endpoint values and supposeApply the same inverse to this residual and add the correction to .\nThe result satisfies the exact forced equation and boundary values, with\nvalue error at most  and first-derivative\nerror at most . Thus a tighter residual\ncertificate improves both error bounds through the same theorem.The inverse also gives a Cartesian field. For positive radius and\n, the inverse polar Jacobian proves  regularity of\nthe reconstructed streamfunction. The local chart round trip then givesThis excludes the origin and the chart's negative horizontal cut. If the\nforcing is odd about , then the solution is odd there too:\n and . Uniqueness follows from the conserved\nenergy of the difference of two oscillator solutions with the same initial\nvalue and slope. These are tangential angular identities; the normal extension\nof the source vorticity can jump at the physical boundary and needs a separate\nweak argument.\n\nDifferentiate the two integrals by the fundamental theorem of calculus.\nThe terms involving  cancel in the first derivative and combine to\n in the second, giving the forced equation. At the left endpoint both\nintegrals vanish; the chosen amplitude cancels the value at the right\nendpoint. Bounding sine and cosine by one bounds each integral by \nand gives the displayed estimate. The homogeneous polar identity then\nsupplies the radial factor.\n\nLet  and\n. If a  streamfunction is odd under\n, thenand therefore  while . If in addition\n, then . Thus the scalar symmetries together\nwith odd streamfunction parity and the Dirichlet trace imply all velocity\nconditions used by the normalization calculation.\n\nDifferentiate . Tangential differentiation introduces\none sign from  and one from oddness, while normal differentiation\nintroduces only the sign from oddness. The boundary identity follows by\ndifferentiating the zero trace tangentially.\n\nDynamic rescaling leaves the rates  undetermined. The\npaper fixes two of them by requiring the origin traces  and\n to stay constant in time, the third being the compatibility\nlaw , and the symmetry bridge supplies the velocity\nconditions this calculation uses. Because division is totalized in Lean, the\nnondegeneracy  that makes the quotient meaningful is carried\nas an explicit hypothesis.\n\nAt the origin write ,\n, and . The normalization\niswith the necessary condition . The accompanying symmetry\npredicates distinguish the minimal cancellations used at the origin from the\nstreamfunction-level assumptions that imply them.\n\nWhether the traces are in fact preserved is decided by their evolution\nequations. Differentiating the rescaled vorticity equation once and the\ntemperature equation twice in  and evaluating at the origin, the symmetry\nconditions remove the normal and undifferentiated transport terms and leave two\nlinear scalar ODEs in which the rates appear as coefficients.\n\nFor sufficiently smooth space-time fields satisfying the rescaled equations\non the open upper half-plane and the stated symmetries,These are derived from equation (2.8) by differentiating in the tangential\ndirection, extending the differentiated identities continuously to the\norigin, and commuting space and time derivatives. The fields are represented\nby smooth extensions across the boundary, but the equations are assumed only\non the open upper half-plane.\n\nDifferentiate the vorticity equation once and the temperature equation twice\nin the tangential variable. At the origin, parity and the two zero traces\nremove the normal and undifferentiated transport terms. Expanding the remaining\nadvection products gives the two displayed scalar ODEs.\n\nSubstitution of the three normalization identities makes both trace-ODE\nright-hand sides zero. Hence, on an open connected time domain where the\nregularity, upper-half-plane equations, symmetries, and normalization hold at every\ntime,\n\nThe first normalization identity implies\n. Together with the formulas for\n and , elementary algebra cancels both ODE right-hand\nsides. A differentiable real-valued function whose derivative vanishes on an\nopen connected set is constant there.\n\nConstant rates make the rescaled equations autonomous; a stationary profile\nis an additional condition. The fixed-power construction starts with\ntime-independent primitive profiles and a single exponent .\nThe exponents of  and  are forced by requiring buoyancy and\npressure to balance . This ansatz is checked against the\nprimitive classical predicate rather than the vorticity-stream model.\n\nFor an exponent  and profiles , setThe ansatz is an instance of the common backward-similarity transformation.\nThe quotient formula is used analytically only for , where the real-power\nbase is positive.\n\nSubstituting the ansatz into the Boussinesq system, every term of the momentum\nequation carries the factor , every term of the temperature\nequation carries , and time differentiation of the profiles\nproduces the scaling terms  and\n. Dividing out the powers\nleaves a stationary system in the similarity variable.\n\nThe stationary profile equations areThe profile predicate also requires global first-order classical regularity;\nthe residual equalities alone would otherwise use totalized derivatives.\n\nThe reduction is exact rather than asymptotic: each physical residual is a\nnonzero power of  times the corresponding profile residual, so for \nvanishing of one is equivalent to vanishing of the other. The direction checked\nhere goes from profile to physical solution, including the transfer of\nregularity.\n\nEvery classical profile satisfying the reduced equations generates a classical\nsolution of the physical Boussinesq system on any time set contained in\n.\n\nDifferentiate the backward ansatz using the common similarity chain rule.\nVelocity time differentiation, convection, pressure gradient, and buoyancy all\ncarry the factor . Temperature time differentiation and\ntemperature advection both carry , while divergence carries\n. The three physical residuals are therefore these nonzero factors\ntimes the three reduced residuals. The regularity part follows by composing the\n profiles with the smooth positive backward power scales on .\n\nThe zero velocity, temperature, and pressure profiles satisfy the reduced\nequations and generate the zero classical solution for every .\n\nAll profile derivatives and nonlinear terms vanish. Applying the exact\nreduction theorem gives the physical solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Euler, Symmetry, and Boussinesq Flow","header":"8.4.2. Inviscid Boussinesq Flow","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Inviscid-Boussinesq-Flow"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--The-Gaussian-Heat-Kernel--The-Gaussian-Kernel--Example___-Verifying-the-Heat-Equation":{"contents":"For  and , the kernel is\n\n\n\nTo verify  by hand, compute each\nside. Write  with .\nThen\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe two expressions coincide term by term. Heat scaling determines the\nquadratic exponent  and the mass-preserving prefactor; their time\nand spatial derivatives then cancel in exactly the displayed combination.\n\nPhysically, the spreading width  grows as the square\nroot of time. This is the diffusive scaling: doubling the observation time\nincreases the spread by a factor of , not . Strict positivity\nof the Gaussian makes the solution from nonnegative, nonzero continuous data\npositive everywhere at every positive time\n(), while the influence at distance\n is exponentially small in . This gives the\nparabolic equation infinite propagation speed with exponentially small\nfar-field influence.\n\nFor  and , the heat kernel isThe Lean function is total: it is defined to be zero when .\nThe differential identities for it assume .\n\nThe worked one-dimensional computation is the case  of the general\nidentity. In dimension  the prefactor  contributes\n to the logarithmic time derivative, and the  diagonal entries\nof the Gaussian Hessian each contribute  to the Laplacian, so the\ntwo sides agree in every dimension with no change to the exponent.\n\nAt every positive time, the Gaussian satisfies the homogeneous heat equation\npointwise:The identity holds in every finite spatial dimension .\n\nDifferentiate the Gaussian time factor and exponential factor, compute each\nspatial second derivative, and sum the diagonal Hessian entries. Both sides\nreduce to\n.\n\nThe differential identity verifies the Gaussian formula but does not fix its\nmultiplicative constant: every constant multiple also solves the heat equation. The prefactor is chosen so that the spatial integral equals one, and\nit is this normalization, not the equation, that the convolution estimates\nfor the initial-value problem depend on.\n\nFor every , the heat kernel has unit spatial mass:This is the normalization needed for preservation of constants and for the\napproximate-identity argument.\n\nApply Mathlib's Gaussian integral with coefficient . The spatial\nintegral of the exponential is ; multiplying by the\nprefactor  gives one.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Classical Heat Flow\u0009The Gaussian Heat Kernel\u0009The Gaussian Kernel","header":"6.2.2.1.1. Example: Verifying the Heat Equation","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--The-Gaussian-Heat-Kernel--The-Gaussian-Kernel--Example___-Verifying-the-Heat-Equation"}});